    <h2 id="s1">The short answer</h2>
    <p>There is no such thing as a heli-ski resort in the way there is a ski resort. Heli-skiing has <strong>no lifts, no pistes and no resort village</strong>. Instead you are based at a <strong>lodge or hotel</strong> and flown out by helicopter each day to untracked backcountry terrain that no lift could ever reach. When people search for heli-ski resorts, what they are really looking for is heli-ski <strong>lodges</strong> and the regions around them.</p>
    <p>One of the finest of those regions is the <strong>Troll Peninsula in North Iceland</strong>, where the base is the four-star Sigló Hótel and the terrain runs sea-to-summit from Arctic ridgelines to the ocean. Heliski Travel arranges trips there end to end, and as the authorised agent, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct.</p>

    <h2 id="s2">Why there is no heli-ski resort</h2>
    <p>The reason is simple: heli-skiing exists precisely to escape the resort. A resort is defined by infrastructure, lifts, groomed pistes, marked runs and the crowds that share them. Heli-skiing removes all of that. The helicopter is the lift, the mountain is the run, and the snow is untracked because no one else can get there.</p>
    <p>So the concept of a "heli-ski resort" is a contradiction. What operators build instead is a <strong>base of comfort</strong>, a lodge to sleep, eat and recover in, paired with access to a vast area of backcountry terrain. The magic is in the absence of resort infrastructure, not the presence of it.</p>

    <h2 id="s3">How heli-skiing works instead</h2>
    <p>If there is no resort, how does a trip actually run? Like this:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You stay at a lodge</strong>, your comfortable base for the trip.</li>
      <li><strong>Each morning a helicopter flies you out</strong> to untracked terrain chosen by the guide for the day's conditions.</li>
      <li><strong>A certified guide leads your small group</strong> down long descents, while the helicopter meets you at the bottom and lifts you back up.</li>
      <li><strong>You ski untracked snow all day</strong>, typically 15,000 to 25,000 vertical feet, then fly home to the lodge.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It is lift-served skiing with the lift replaced by an aircraft and the crowds removed entirely. For a full walk through the day, see our guide to <a href="/field-notes/heli-skiing-tours">how heli-skiing tours work</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s4">The heli-ski lodge</h2>
    <p>The closest thing to a "resort" in heli-skiing is the lodge, and a good one matters as much as the terrain. It is where the day begins and ends, and a comfortable, well-run base transforms the trip. Some heli regions use remote wilderness lodges reached only by air; others use hotels in characterful towns.</p>
    <p>In Iceland, the base is the <strong>four-star Sigló Hótel</strong> in <strong>Siglufjörður</strong>, built onto the marina in a former herring town. It is a short helicopter hop from the terrain, so more of your day is spent skiing, and it offers proper rooms, a restaurant and a harbour-side hot tub. It is the antidote to the idea that leaving the resort means roughing it. Read more on <a href="/field-notes/the-heliski-lodge-experience">the lodge experience</a> and the <a href="/siglo-hotel">Sigló Hótel</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s5">Where you can heli-ski</h2>
    <p>Heli-skiing takes place in a handful of great backcountry regions around the world, each served by lodges and operators rather than resorts:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Canada</strong>, the birthplace of commercial heli-skiing, with vast interior ranges.</li>
      <li><strong>Alaska</strong>, famous for its big, steep, committing spines.</li>
      <li><strong>The Alps</strong>, where heli access is limited but spectacular where allowed.</li>
      <li><strong>The Caucasus, the Andes and New Zealand</strong>, each with their own character and season.</li>
      <li><strong>Iceland</strong>, whose Troll Peninsula offers rare sea-to-summit descents and long spring daylight.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you are weighing them up, our comparisons of <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-iceland-vs-alaska">Iceland versus Alaska</a>, <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-iceland-vs-canada">Iceland versus Canada</a> and <a href="/field-notes/best-heliskiing-destinations-world">the best destinations in the world</a> lay out the trade-offs.</p>

    <h2 id="s6">Resort skiing vs heli-skiing</h2>
    <p>The two are different experiences, and neither replaces the other. Resort skiing is convenient, sociable and endlessly repeatable: lifts, pistes, apres and easy access. It is the backbone of most people's ski lives, and rightly so.</p>
    <p>Heli-skiing is the opposite in the best way: exclusive, wild and untracked, with no lift lines and no crowds, but weather-dependent and premium in price. Where a resort sells you a lift pass, heli-skiing sells you <strong>guaranteed vertical feet</strong>, so weather delays are the operator's cost, not yours. For a fuller comparison, read <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-vs-resort-skiing">heli-skiing versus resort skiing</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s7">Who it suits</h2>
    <p>Because there are no green runs or gentle pistes, heli-skiing is not for absolute beginners. You should be a <strong>competent, confident intermediate</strong> who can ski most red terrain off-piste in variable snow. Within that, guides choose terrain to suit the group, so you do not need to be an expert, but you do need solid off-piste skills.</p>
    <p>If that describes you, and you have ever looked beyond the resort ropes and wanted more, this is the natural next step. If you are close but not quite there, read <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-for-beginners">heli-skiing for beginners</a> first.</p>

    <h2 id="s8">What it costs</h2>
    <p>Without lift passes, heli-skiing is priced as a package. Iceland trips run <strong>roughly EUR 3,490 to EUR 82,990</strong>, spanning three, four and five-day weeks in shared, semi-private and private formats. The format moves the price far more than the dates, and most guests sit in the middle.</p>
    <p>The pricing is honest about weather: trips are sold by guaranteed vertical feet, so delays and repositioning are the operator's cost, not yours. For a full breakdown, see <a href="/heliskiing-guide/heliskiing-cost-iceland">what heli-skiing costs in Iceland</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s9">How to book</h2>
    <p>Since there is no resort website to book a lift pass, the simplest route is through a specialist. Tell Heliski Travel your group, your level and your preferred dates, and we will match you to the right lodge, region and format, and arrange it end to end. As the <strong>authorised booking agent for Viking Heliskiing</strong>, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct, and we reply within 12 hours.</p>
    <p>When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a>, see the terrain on the <a href="/iceland">Iceland page</a>, or <a href="/contact">request a tailored quote</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s10">Do any resorts offer heli-skiing?</h2>
    <p>A few ski resorts around the world do sit near heli operations, and this is probably where the idea of a "heli-ski resort" comes from. In parts of the Alps, for example, heli-drops are permitted at a small number of designated points, so a resort-based skier can add a heli run or two. In North America, some resort towns are also home to heli and cat operators working the surrounding backcountry.</p>
    <p>But these are still <strong>separate operations</strong>, not a resort lift you ride. The heli-skiing happens away from the pistes, with its own guides, its own safety systems and its own pricing. So while you can sometimes combine a resort holiday with a heli day, you are booking two different things. If your priority is the heli-skiing itself, a dedicated heli region such as Iceland's Troll Peninsula gives you far more of it, and better terrain, than a resort with an occasional heli-drop.</p>

    <h2 id="s11">The closest thing to a resort week</h2>
    <p>If what you really want is the ease of a resort holiday, arrive, settle in, ski every day, come home, a <strong>lodge-based heli week</strong> delivers exactly that, minus the lifts and crowds. You base yourself at one comfortable hotel, and each day the skiing comes to you by helicopter rather than you queuing for a lift.</p>
    <p>In Iceland that means a week at the <strong>four-star Sigló Hótel</strong>, skiing the Troll Peninsula by day and returning to the same room, restaurant and hot tub each night. It has the rhythm and comfort of a resort holiday with none of the compromises, no lift lines, no tracked-out afternoons, no sharing the mountain. For many people it is the best of both worlds, and it is why a heli week feels so effortless once you are there. See the <a href="/field-notes/the-heliski-lodge-experience">lodge experience</a> for a fuller picture.</p>

    <h2 id="s12">What a heli week gives you that a resort cannot</h2>
    <p>Strip the comparison to its essentials and the case for a heli week over a resort holiday is straightforward. Every run is on <strong>untracked snow</strong>, not chopped-up afternoon piste. There are <strong>no lift lines and no crowds</strong>, because the only people on your mountain are your own group. And the terrain is genuinely out of reach of any lift, big open faces and long descents that finish near the sea.</p>
    <p>You also get things a resort cannot sell at any price: a <strong>certified guide devoted to your small group</strong>, <strong>guaranteed vertical feet</strong> that protect your budget from the weather, and the quiet luxury of a mountain to yourselves. A resort holiday is convenient and sociable, and there is a place for it. But if the goal is the finest skiing of your life on snow no one else has touched, a heli week is a different order of experience. When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a>.</p>

